Level 1 (Startup/Fresher)
1. What is an abstract class?
Answer

An abstract class is a class declared with the abstract keyword.
It cannot be instantiated and is mainly used to provide a common base class with both complete and incomplete (abstract) methods.

abstract class Animal{

    abstract void sound();

    void eat(){
        System.out.println("Eating");
    }
}
2. Can we create an object of an abstract class?
Answer

No.

Animal a = new Animal(); // Compile-time error

Reason:

Abstract classes may contain abstract methods, so Java prevents creating incomplete objects.

3. Can an abstract class have constructors?
Answer

Yes.

abstract class Animal{

    Animal(){
        System.out.println("Animal Constructor");
    }
}

The constructor is called when a subclass object is created.

4. Why does an abstract class have a constructor if we cannot create its object?
Answer

Because constructors initialize inherited fields.

Example

abstract class Animal{

    Animal(){
        System.out.println("Animal Constructor");
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal{

}

Output

Animal Constructor

The constructor initializes the parent part of the Dog object.

